;; itonami.cloud deployment profile.
;;
;; What this profile is FOR is the one thing the tenant-neutral distribution
;; cannot ship: a working sign-in-link delivery endpoint.
;;
;; ADR-0012 designed email sign-in as an alternate session proof and left
;; delivery to "a deployment-owned HTTPS endpoint". No such endpoint existed
;; anywhere in the fleet, so `:email-login` stayed `:enabled? false` and the
;; form was never shown — the feature was complete and unreachable. The
;; endpoint now exists (`cloud-itonami.edge.email-login/on-request-post-deliver`
;; on itonami.cloud, backed by that deployment's Resend sender), and this
;; profile is what points at it.
;;
;; It stays out of the shipped defaults on purpose. A tenant-neutral install
;; that emailed through somebody else's sending reputation would be making a
;; choice on the operator's behalf, and a bounce or a spam complaint would land
;; on a domain they do not own.
;;
;;   CLOUD_ITONAMI_EMAIL_LOGIN_TOKEN=<bearer>  CLOUD_ITONAMI_PROFILE=itonami \
;;     clojure -M:server
;;
;; The bearer must equal the endpoint's `MAGIC_LINK_DELIVERY_TOKEN` Worker
;; secret. It is read from the environment at call time and never persisted —
;; `cloud.itonami.app.email-login/access-token` reads it per request and the
;; adapter refuses to run without it.
;;
;; This deployment allows a verified Email or provider subject to create a
;; personal User. Matching Email addresses are never used to merge identities:
;; an existing User must sign in first and explicitly link the provider.
;; Payment, signature, federation and outward-authority approval continue to
;; use their explicit Passkey ceremonies.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Bots run on OpenRouter's free plan (owner instruction, 2026-08-27).
;;
;; This is the operator's act of reviewing, which is the only place it can
;; happen: `resources/cloud-itonami-app.defaults.edn` ships every destination
;; that leaves the machine as `:reviewed? false :enabled? false`, because a
;; tenant-neutral distribution cannot consent to sending somebody's Bot
;; transcript anywhere. This deployment can, and does, for itself.
;;
;; What is being consented to, stated plainly because the second half is easy
;; to miss: the transcript goes to OpenRouter, AND onward to whichever third
;; party serves the free endpoint that minute — a different company on
;; different turns. Which endpoints are offered at all is decided by this
;; account's data policy at openrouter.ai/settings/privacy, not here.
;;
;; `openrouter/free` is a router, not a model. Measured 2026-08-27 it answered
;; a tool-call request with HTTP 200 and .model =
;; "nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning:free". Pinning a free model id instead would
;; be ADR-2608270100's failure one tier down, on the fastest-moving set of ids
;; this workspace touches — see root ADR-2608272100.
;;
;; The fleet stays in the catalog and stays reviewable. It is not the default
;; any more; it is what an operator picks explicitly.
;;
;;   OPENROUTER_API_KEY=<key> CLOUD_ITONAMI_PROFILE=itonami clojure -M:server
;;
;; The key is read from the environment by `config/env-secret` at call time and
;; never persisted. On the owner's machine it lives in the login Keychain as
;; `gftd.openrouter`; `provider-allowed?` derives "authenticated" from the
;; variable actually being set, so a deployment that forgets to export it gets
;; a refused provider rather than a silent fallback.
{:auth {:allow-signup? true
        :sso-enabled? false
        :sso-providers []}
 :cloud-relay {:base-url "https://hooks.itonami.cloud"
               :access-token-env "CLOUD_ITONAMI_RELAY_ACCESS_TOKEN"}
 ;; The Bots' unattended workforce turns run on the router too, not on a
 ;; pinned free id. `:workforce :model` is what a scheduled Bot asks for; the
 ;; provider above is what serves it.
 :bots {:mail-domain "mail.itonami.cloud"
        :workforce {:model "openrouter/free"}}
 :email-login
 {:enabled? true
  :delivery-endpoint "https://itonami.cloud/api/auth/magic-link/deliver"
  :access-token-env "CLOUD_ITONAMI_EMAIL_LOGIN_TOKEN"}
 :providers [{:id "openrouter" :enabled? true :reviewed? true}]
 :routing {:default-provider "openrouter"
           :default-model "openrouter/free"
           :cloud-enabled? true}}
